WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.160
John Constantine has a long history across every&nbsp;
version of DC’s expansive universe. But what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.920
are his true origins? And was he actually the&nbsp;
reason Swamp Thing was able to have a daughter?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.000
Stay tuned to find out!

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.200
Before we get into the in-universe history of&nbsp;
DC's unluckiest mystic, it's worth taking a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.160
look at the behind-the-scenes story of how he&nbsp;
came to be. After all, while most of the UK's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.200
famous fictional spellcasters descend from the&nbsp;
tradition of Merlin, our boy John was inspired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.840
by a very different British hero: Gordon&nbsp;
Sumner, better known to the world as Sting.&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:40.080
The story behind John Constantine's first&nbsp;
appearance in Swamp Thing #37 is pretty simple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:45.280
When writer Alan Moore asked artists Steve&nbsp;
Bissette and John Totleben what they’d like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.520
to draw, they told him they wanted to draw&nbsp;
Sting or at least someone who looked like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.760
him. Moore thought Sting's striking&nbsp;
look would work for the charismatic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:59.040
manipulative magician that he had in mind, and&nbsp;
thus, John Constantine stepped onto the page.&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.360
As for the specific visual references that&nbsp;
Bissette and Totleben were drawing from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.760
the long coat and suit look that would become John&nbsp;
Constantine's trademark was inspired by Sting's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:13.440
appearance in 1979's Quadrophenia — based on the&nbsp;
Who album of the same name. Another inspiration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:19.840
though, was much more esoteric: a 1982 TV movie&nbsp;
called Brimstone &amp; Treacle, where Sting played a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.400
disturbing, fourth-wall-breaking character who may&nbsp;
or may not have been the devil. Pretty fitting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.320
all things considered.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:31.280
Saying that John Constantine has had a rough&nbsp;
life is putting things pretty mildly. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:36.000
very few characters in comics who've had quite&nbsp;
as much misery heaped on them over the years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.360
In John's case, as revealed in Hellblazer&nbsp;
#39, it started even before he was born.&nbsp;

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:46.080
As a child, John occasionally had visions&nbsp;
of what he called the "Golden Boy." He was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:50.880
essentially just that: an "imaginary friend" made&nbsp;
up of shining golden light that John both loved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.840
and hated for his seeming perfection. After he&nbsp;
grew up, though, John learned that he was neither&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:01.120
imaginary nor a friend but a parallel world echo&nbsp;
of the twin brother he never even knew he had.&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:06.160
The existence of a twin wasn't just news to him;&nbsp;
the doctors who delivered John as a baby were also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:11.520
pretty surprised when it turned out that Mary Anne&nbsp;
Constantine had been pregnant with a second child.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.720
The other brother was so sickly&nbsp;
and underweight that he'd gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.480
unnoticed. The healthier child didn't make&nbsp;
it, though. He'd been strangled in the womb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:24.320
and neither he nor Mary survived the birth. The&nbsp;
other baby was, of course, John. His childhood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:28.880
was marked by intense hatred and resentment from&nbsp;
his father, who made it very clear that he wished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.840
John had been the one who'd died instead.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.840
John and his father, Thomas Constantine,&nbsp;
didn't get along while John was growing up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.800
Not only did Thomas blame John for Mary's&nbsp;
death, but he was also disappointed in him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.120
for other reasons. Thomas wanted a "normal"&nbsp;
son who would fit in with the other kids at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.840
school rather than a young weirdo who spent&nbsp;
all his time on books about magic and demons.&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.400
The elder Constantine burned a stack&nbsp;
of his son's books in a fit of anger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.320
hoping to drive him away from sorcery. This&nbsp;
might've worked out for the best, but John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.840
was furious. He decided to get back at his dad, so&nbsp;
he cursed him by binding his soul to a dead cat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:09.680
Before long, Thomas was wasting away from an&nbsp;
inexplicable disease as the feline corpse rotted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:14.720
but John was left feeling guilty about what he'd&nbsp;
done to his old man. Since the spell couldn't be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:20.400
reversed, he preserved the cat in formaldehyde,&nbsp;
stopping his father from degenerating any further.&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.360
Cut to a few decades later, and Thomas finally&nbsp;
died, only for his soul to be stuck on Earth. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.640
terrified John's niece Gemma until John realized&nbsp;
that as long as the preserved cat was around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:34.960
Thomas would be bound by it and would&nbsp;
continue suffering. To free him, John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:39.760
dug up the cat from under his mother’s tombstone&nbsp;
where he'd buried it. He then burned the corpse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.680
finally setting his father free.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:45.440
If John Constantine has an origin story&nbsp;
in the traditional superhero sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:49.840
then it's what went down with the Newcastle&nbsp;
Crew's disastrous attempt to help a young girl.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:54.640
The gory details are in Hellblazer #11,&nbsp;
and they are not for the faint of heart.&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.800
It happens early on in John's career as a&nbsp;
freelance magician when he hears about some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.920
bad stuff going down at the Casanova Club. The&nbsp;
club's owner was involved with the same sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:08.480
shady magic scene as John, but his proclivities&nbsp;
were far more disturbing, including the abuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.840
of his daughter, Astra Logue. Eventually, Astra&nbsp;
manifested a monster called the "Norfulthing." And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.040
after being summoned, the Norfulthing does to the&nbsp;
club owner and his "guests" exactly what they did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.720
to Astra. It also tore off their heads and turned&nbsp;
the basement of the Casanova into a charnel house.&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:28.080
To deal with the Norfulthing,&nbsp;
John gathered six other occultists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.880
to form the Newcastle Crew. From there, he came&nbsp;
up with the not-so-brilliant plan of summoning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.080
a powerful demon to kill Astra's monster.
"When John found out what was happening with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:45.280
girl, he decided that he was going to save Astra."
It did not go well. While the ritual was correct,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:48.720
Constantine got the wrong name for the&nbsp;
demon. Since the name is the thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.160
provided the control, he essentially&nbsp;
unleashed a horrifying and hungry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:57.680
force on himself, his friends, and poor Astra.
The demon, Nergal, did destroy the Norfulthing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:03.120
but it then turned on John and his friends. John&nbsp;
was forced to lead Astra through Hell itself in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:07.040
an attempt to save her soul. And while he&nbsp;
held tight to her hand for the whole trip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:11.600
said hand didn't stay attached to the rest of the&nbsp;
girl. She was dragged to Hell, and the rest of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:16.640
Newcastle Crew was left traumatized. They all met&nbsp;
grisly fates before their souls were condemned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:21.680
Hell. As for John, he managed to avoid eternal&nbsp;
damnation, but he wound up suffering his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:23.920
personal hell for the next few years.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:28.720
The contingency plan for summoning the demon at&nbsp;
the Casanova was one that we can probably all get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:34.080
behind: blowing it up with a very enthusiastic&nbsp;
round of arson. Unfortunately for John,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:38.800
setting things on fire tends to be very effective&nbsp;
at both solving problems and creating them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:43.840
When this bizarre magician emerged from a fire,&nbsp;
clutching a severed hand and ranting about a demon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:49.040
that dragged him to Hell, he wound up being taken&nbsp;
to the Ravenscar Secure Hospital — a miserable and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:53.840
disreputable asylum for the criminally insane.
The chief problem for Constantine was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:58.640
while he was certainly traumatized, he wasn't&nbsp;
hallucinating. There was a demon that was after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:03.600
his soul. But the only thing that the staff and&nbsp;
inmates of Ravenscar knew was that all this bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:09.200
stuff happened and John was involved. As a result,&nbsp;
Constantine was beaten by the inmates and tortured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:14.320
by the doctors under the guise of "treatment,"&nbsp;
with electro-convulsive "therapy" being a favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:19.680
tactic. All of this only served to amplify his&nbsp;
self-loathing and the guilt he felt for his plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:25.040
going so horrifically wrong. As Hellblazer #50&nbsp;
put it in one of its typically cheerful moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:30.000
"The only reason he hated them was that they&nbsp;
wouldn't cut his throat and end the agony."&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.040
John's tenure at Ravenscar lasted two years. But&nbsp;
the memory of both the events that led him there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:38.640
and the treatment he received in its walls&nbsp;
would haunt him for the rest of his life.&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:41.120
Like a lot of comic book characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:46.000
there are different versions of John Constantine's&nbsp;
backstory depending on what comics you're reading,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:50.640
but his path is weirder than most. When he was&nbsp;
introduced in the pages of Swamp Thing, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:56.320
as part of the DC Universe. Most of his series,&nbsp;
however, was published by DC's Vertigo imprint — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:01.120
line initially focused on horror titles for&nbsp;
adult readers that was separated from the bright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:06.080
superheroic world of the main DC comics.&nbsp;
Eventually, after about 20 years of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:11.760
sequestered in his own supernatural universe,&nbsp;
Constantine was folded back into the DCU proper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:16.000
His origin was then revised, with a little&nbsp;
less of the disturbing darkness involved.&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.560
The new origin story that led Constantine to&nbsp;
join the Justice League Dark and deal with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:26.160
DC Universe's magical threats still wound its&nbsp;
way through Newcastle, Astra Logue's damnation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:31.440
and a stint in Ravenscar Asylum. This time,&nbsp;
though, things started to go bad much earlier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.920
In Constantine #14, readers learned that&nbsp;
at a very young age, John encountered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:41.040
some sinister demonic force that offered him&nbsp;
magical power. John eagerly accepted, but as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:44.640
is usually the case with this sort of thing,&nbsp;
he wasn't told about the price of this power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:49.680
until it was too late to do anything about it.
The price, in typically tragic fashion, was John's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:55.280
home and family. In his first use of magic,&nbsp;
Constantine burned down his childhood house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:58.400
standing untouched in the center of the&nbsp;
flames while his mother and father were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.320
burned to death in front of him.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:04.320
There is one element of Constantine's&nbsp;
pre-trenchcoat life that's consistent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:09.360
among various versions of the character. Before&nbsp;
he started skulking around in his iconic duster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:13.120
manipulating swamp monsters into doing&nbsp;
his mysterious and vaguely sinister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:17.120
bidding, he was into some really&nbsp;
disreputable stuff: rock 'n' roll.&nbsp;

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:21.600
In his younger days, Constantine, under the&nbsp;
not-so-great stage name "Johnny Con-Job," was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:26.560
the lead singer in a punk band called Mucous&nbsp;
Membrane. According to Constantine himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:30.480
they weren't very good when it came to the&nbsp;
actual music. But their brief time in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:36.080
grotesque spotlights of British punk clubs did see&nbsp;
them find some success. Of course, that had a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:41.040
less to do with their musical talent than it did&nbsp;
with the undeniable charisma of their frontman.&nbsp;

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:45.920
The reason that Mucous Membrane broke up varies&nbsp;
from one series to the next. In the current DC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:51.200
Universe, it was prompted by Constantine's use&nbsp;
of magic as part of his stagecraft. It caused his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:56.240
bandmate and lover, Veronica Delacroix, to fade&nbsp;
away from the physical world until she became a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:01.920
disembodied spirit. In the classic Vertigo comics,&nbsp;
it had more to do with the Newcastle incident and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:07.200
John's subsequent commitment to Ravenscar. At one&nbsp;
point, an amateur documentary crew trying to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:13.600
out what happened to Mucous Membrane starred in&nbsp;
a story that ran through Hellblazer #245 to 246.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:18.960
In this arc, they were all violently murdered&nbsp;
by the remnants of Astra's terror elemental,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:20.400
the Norfulthing.

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:25.120
Another one of the more consistent elements in&nbsp;
Constantine's life is his romantic history with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:29.280
stage magician, legitimate sorceress,&nbsp;
and Justice Leaguer, Zatanna Zatara.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:35.040
The backward-talking spellcaster predates John's&nbsp;
portrayal in comics by 20 years, but their pasts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.120
have been depicted as being intertwined&nbsp;
since very early in Constantine's existence.&nbsp;

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:43.280
"Zee, are you alright?"
"Yeah, as soon as you get off me."&nbsp;

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:48.000
"I should have known you were the reason&nbsp;
I'm not back in London enjoying a pint."&nbsp;

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:52.320
Sadly, the way their lives are tangled together&nbsp;
is about as tragic as everything else in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:56.880
Constantine's past. While they do maintain&nbsp;
some level of affection for each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:01.120
there is a pretty big sticking point in their&nbsp;
history that prompted their initial breakup:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:06.320
Constantine killed her dad.
He didn't intentionally end John Zatara's life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:10.880
but he was the driving force behind a magic&nbsp;
ritual that resulted in her father bursting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:15.600
into flames and dying in front of them. In the&nbsp;
original comics, this was an attempt to deal with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:20.400
the mystical side of the multiverse-shattering&nbsp;
events seen in Crisis on Infinite Earths. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:24.800
a similar event exists even in the current&nbsp;
continuity where the Crisis didn't happen.&nbsp;

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:28.720
Before they broke up, Zatanna cast a spell&nbsp;
on John, telling him "You need to be a better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:32.720
person," though of course, she said it in her&nbsp;
backward way of spellcasting. That would explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:37.200
why Constantine is less of a loner and a bit&nbsp;
more eager to help these days, wouldn't it?

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.480
Constantine was originally introduced as a&nbsp;
supporting character for Swamp Thing, and while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:47.280
he's had plenty of success on his own, he's always&nbsp;
had that association. There's another reason that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:52.160
those characters will always be inextricably&nbsp;
linked, though. Believe it or not, the saga&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:57.360
of Swamp Thing is, at its heart, a romance&nbsp;
between the eponymous muck monster and his wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:02.400
Abby Arcane. At one point, after the creation of&nbsp;
a "seed" that would combine Swampy's plant-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:06.640
essence with a human soul, Abby and the Swamp&nbsp;
Thing decided to have a child of their own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:12.080
The problem was that Swamp Thing was not human&nbsp;
and didn’t have the right…equipment, for the job.&nbsp;

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:15.680
What he did have was the ability to&nbsp;
take over someone else's body using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:20.640
semi-mystical plant spores and a person who&nbsp;
owed him several favors. With that in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:25.040
Swamp Thing took over John Constantine's&nbsp;
body, flew to Louisiana, and then used him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:30.480
impregnate Abby with their daughter, Tefe. After&nbsp;
Constantine manipulated him into several dubious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:35.600
semi-sinister errands of his own, Swamp Thing felt&nbsp;
justified in his actions as a means to an end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:40.560
The tree that Swamp Thing got tattooed on&nbsp;
Constantine's rear end while he was piloting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:43.191
the guy's body, however, was pure revenge.

